WEBVTT

00:00:00.676 --> 00:00:02.526
Command of evidence.

00:00:02.526 --> 00:00:04.491
Split the claim into parts.

00:00:04.491 --> 00:00:06.459
The evidence has to hit every part.

00:00:08.014 --> 00:00:09.879
Here's how to spot one.

00:00:09.879 --> 00:00:13.584
The passage gives a claim, or a hypothesis.

00:00:13.584 --> 00:00:18.125
And the question asks which finding would most directly support it, or weaken it.

00:00:19.160 --> 00:00:21.707
It's testing what actually counts as evidence.

00:00:22.769 --> 00:00:24.269
Here's the method.

00:00:24.269 --> 00:00:26.064
Find the exact claim.

00:00:26.064 --> 00:00:29.939
It's often the last sentence, or the hypothesis.

00:00:29.939 --> 00:00:31.849
Split it into its parts.

00:00:31.849 --> 00:00:35.439
Decide: do you need support, or weaken?

00:00:35.439 --> 00:00:38.971
Then pick the choice that hits every part, in the right direction.

00:00:40.061 --> 00:00:41.986
Four evidence rules.

00:00:41.986 --> 00:00:45.901
Support means the result the claim predicts actually happens.

00:00:45.901 --> 00:00:51.381
Weaken means the same result with a different cause, or the cause with no result.

00:00:51.381 --> 00:00:53.461
Half the claim isn't enough.

00:00:53.461 --> 00:00:55.021
Hit every part.

00:00:55.021 --> 00:00:58.737
And for quotes from poems or novels, the literal meaning wins.

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:01.245
Let's try one.

00:01:01.245 --> 00:01:07.170
The hypothesis is that the lizards developed larger toe pads since the hurricane in twenty seventeen.

00:01:07.170 --> 00:01:11.795
It has three parts: toe pads, bigger, and after the storm.

00:01:11.795 --> 00:01:13.890
Choice A is about diet.

00:01:13.890 --> 00:01:16.500
Choice C is about other species.

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:19.955
Choice D only confirms that the winds were strong.

00:01:19.955 --> 00:01:22.790
None of them show that these lizards changed.

00:01:22.790 --> 00:01:27.464
Choice B shows bigger toe pads after the hurricane, so it hits every part.

00:01:28.539 --> 00:01:31.689
Watch out for the right topic with the wrong claim.

00:01:31.689 --> 00:01:36.759
A choice can talk about the same lizards, or the same storm, and still prove nothing.

00:01:37.814 --> 00:01:44.909
Here's the whole lesson on one page: how to spot it, the four steps, the evidence rules, and the trap.

00:01:44.909 --> 00:01:47.797
Pause the video now, and copy it into your notes.
